<section class="phero"><div class="phero-media"><img src="/assets/img/photos/porch-home.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/porch-home-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/porch-home.webp 1024w" sizes="100vw" width="1024" height="682" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><a href="/new-hampshire-cities/">Cities</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><span aria-current="page">Rochester</span></nav><p class="kicker">Seacoast · Strafford County</p><h1>Rochester, NH Insurance Appraiser</h1><p class="lede">Rochester stretches from a compact downtown out to woods and back roads, with Gonic and East Rochester holding on to their own village identities. The claims here are practical ones – heat, water, trees and roofs.</p><div class="btns"><a class="btn btn-gold" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-line-light" href="/contact-nh-appraiser/">Free claim review</a></div><ul class="phero-chips"><li><small>County</small><b>Strafford County</b></li><li><small>Region</small><b>Seacoast</b></li><li><small>Housing</small><b>village mill houses, manufactured homes and rural capes on private wells</b></li></ul></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<div class="local-card"><div><h2 class="local-t">At a glance</h2><p><b>Areas we cover:</b> Gonic, East Rochester, downtown Rochester, the Route 125 corridor.</p><p><b>Storm history:</b> The December 2008 ice storm hit Strafford County especially hard, leaving many Rochester households without power for days and setting off a wave of tree-damage and frozen-pipe claims.</p></div><ul class="chips"><li>Ice storms</li><li>Woodstove fires</li><li>Frozen pipes</li><li>Falling trees</li><li>Roof snow load</li></ul></div>

<h2>Downtown, villages and back roads</h2>
<p>Rochester is bigger than it looks from Route 125. The downtown along the Cocheco has a cluster of 19th-century commercial blocks and older homes. Gonic grew around its own woolen mill, and East Rochester sits up near the Salmon Falls River and the state line. Beyond those, a lot of the city is rural: capes and ranches on wooded lots, manufactured-home communities, and houses heated by oil, propane, wood or pellets, drawing water from private wells.</p>
<p>That rural character shapes the losses people bring us.</p>
<h2>When the heat goes out</h2>
<p>A long outage after an ice storm or windstorm is how a lot of Rochester water claims begin. Pipes freeze in an exterior wall or a crawlspace, then split as they thaw. Many policies cover sudden freeze damage if you took reasonable steps to maintain heat – the wording matters, so read it. Disputes tend to follow two lines:</p>
<ol>
<li>Whether the loss is covered at all (an insurer may point to a vacancy or heat-maintenance condition).</li>
<li>How much it costs to dry, open up and rebuild – which is the part appraisal can settle.</li>
</ol>
<p>On the second point, estimates often miss well-pump and pressure-tank work, the cost of reaching pipes in tight crawlspaces and the full drying time a cold house needs. See <a href="/nh-appraisal-services/pipe-burst-water-appraisal/">burst-pipe appraisal</a>.</p>
<h2>Woodstoves and chimney fires</h2>
<p>Wood heat is part of life here, and with it come chimney fires and smoke losses. Even a small fire contained in the flue can push soot through a house and crack a chimney liner. Insurers sometimes scope only the visible damage. A full evaluation looks at the liner, the masonry, the framing around the chimney chase, the HVAC system that carried smoke, and contents in every room. Read more on <a href="/nh-appraisal-services/fire-smoke-soot-appraisal/">fire and smoke appraisal</a>.</p>
<h2>Manufactured homes deserve a real estimate</h2>
<p>Manufactured homes are common around Rochester, and they are often undervalued after a loss. Parts may need to come from specific suppliers, roof and wall systems are different from site-built homes, and transport or setup costs can figure in when replacement is on the table. Many policies for manufactured homes include an appraisal clause just like a standard homeowners form.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Trees on the roof</b>After a storm, photograph the tree in place before removal if it is safe. The way it fell, and what it hit, supports both the debris-removal and the structural parts of a claim.</div>
<p>Private wells and septic systems add another layer out in the rural parts of town. A long outage can mean no water for days, a well pump damaged by a power surge, or a frozen line between the well and the house. Whether those items are covered depends on your policy, but when they are, they belong in the estimate from the start rather than as an afterthought.</p>
<h2>Next steps</h2>
<p>New Hampshire Insurance Appraiser represents Rochester homeowners in appraisal and reviews insurer estimates. To see how your numbers compare, try the <a href="/nh-claim-calculators/depreciation-holdback-planner/">depreciation holdback planner</a>, read <a href="/how-nh-appraisal-works/">how appraisal works</a>, or <a href="/contact-nh-appraiser/">contact us</a>.</p>

<h2>Appraisal help available in Rochester</h2>
<p>Every New Hampshire Insurance Appraiser service is available in Rochester and the surrounding towns, including:</p>
<ul><li><a href="/nh-appraisal-services/appraisal-clause-representation-nh/">Policyholder’s Appraiser on the Panel</a></li><li><a href="/nh-appraisal-services/homeowners-claim-appraisal-nh/">Single-Family Home Claims</a></li><li><a href="/nh-appraisal-services/nh-commercial-hoa-appraisal/">Condo, Association &amp; Business Claims</a></li><li><a href="/nh-appraisal-services/roof-and-hail-appraisal/">Roof Damage &amp; Hail Disputes</a></li><li><a href="/nh-appraisal-services/wind-microburst-tornado-appraisal/">Microburst &amp; Windstorm Losses</a></li><li><a href="/nh-appraisal-services/tropical-storm-hurricane-appraisal-nh/">Tropical Remnants &amp; Coastal Storms</a></li></ul>
<h2>Asked by Rochester owners</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>My pipes froze during a power outage. Will my insurer say I didn’t maintain heat?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Some might raise it. Many policies address freezing in a vacant or unheated home specifically. Whether an outage counts is a coverage question – check your policy and, if needed, talk to an attorney. Appraisal handles the amount once coverage is accepted.</p></div></details><details><summary>Does appraisal work for a manufactured home?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Often, yes. Many manufactured-home policies include an appraisal provision. Read your policy’s wording to confirm.</p></div></details></div>
